<Guide slug="run-two-branches-without-being-there" waMessage={WA_MESSAGES.guideBranches} ctaTitle="Both branches on your phone, every night.">
  <p>
    The second branch is where many good businesses start to struggle. The first shop, restaurant or station works
    because you are in it: you see the queue, the shelves, the drawer and the staff. Then you open a second one across
    town, and suddenly you are only ever in one place. Whichever branch you are not in becomes a guess.
  </p>
  <p>
    You cannot be in two places at once. You can know, every night, what happened in both, and you can make the
    branch you are not in run the same way as the one you are in. Here is a routine that works.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you open: make both branches the same</h2>
  <p>
    Most second-branch problems are differences: different prices, different ways of counting the drawer, a different
    list of who is allowed to do what. Before you worry about watching the new branch, make it a copy of the first.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The same items and prices,</strong> set in one place, so a price change reaches both.</li>
    <li><strong>The same roles.</strong> Decide what a cashier, a supervisor and a branch manager may each do (void a sale, give a discount, receive stock) and give each person exactly that.</li>
    <li><strong>The same close.</strong> Every drawer counted, every night, the same way.</li>
    <li><strong>One person in charge on the spot,</strong> with the authority to act and the knowledge that you will see what they did.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Every night: ten minutes with your phone</h2>
  <p>
    The habit that matters most takes ten minutes. Each evening, look at the same few numbers for each branch, in the
    same order:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Sales,</strong> against the same day last week. A quiet Tuesday is normal; a quiet Saturday is a question.</li>
    <li><strong>Each drawer, over or short,</strong> by name. One short evening is a mistake. The same name short every week is a pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>Voids, refunds and discounts,</strong> by cashier.</li>
    <li><strong>What is running low,</strong> so tomorrow's order is placed tonight, not when the shelf is empty.</li>
  </ol>

  <figure>
    <Phone label="The daily summary on the owner's phone">
      <PhoneSummary />
    </Phone>
    <figcaption>The daily summary email from a demo restaurant: sales, top sellers, each drawer and what is running low.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="guide-zt">
    <p class="guide-zt__h">How ZapTill does it</p>
    <p>
      Every branch shows on one web dashboard that opens on any phone. A daily summary comes by email at the time you
      choose. You can close a branch's day from the web, and see each branch side by side. The ZapTill Cloud is one fee
      for the whole business, KES {kes(PRICES.monthly)} a month however many branches you have, so a second branch adds its
      setup, not a second monthly fee.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Every week: stock and people</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Move stock, don't buy it twice.</strong> One branch's slow mover can be the other's best seller. Before you order, check whether the other branch has too much of it.</li>
    <li><strong>Count the expensive items</strong> in both branches, on the same day.</li>
    <li><strong>Talk to the person in charge</strong> about the week's numbers, with the numbers in front of you both. It changes the conversation from "I think" to "on Thursday".</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In ZapTill, a branch sends a transfer and the other receives it, so both stock levels stay right, and a branch can
    see the other's stock and ask for some. With ZapTill Analytics, the dashboard also suggests, in a plain sentence,
    stock worth moving from one branch to the other.
  </p>

  <h2>Let others see, without giving them your keys</h2>
  <p>
    A business partner wants to see the branch they invested in. Your accountant needs the month's sales. Neither
    should need your password. Give each their own login, limited to what they need: ZapTill has partner and accountant
    logins for exactly this, and a partner sees only the branches you give them.
  </p>

  <h2>Every month: visit without warning</h2>
  <p>
    The numbers tell you where to look; they do not replace looking. Once a month, visit each branch on a day nobody
    expects you. Watch the counter for an hour. Check that the process on the floor is the process you set: drawers
    counted, deliveries checked, voids called to a supervisor. Then compare what you saw with what the dashboard said.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <ProductVideo name="owner" caption={false}
      label="The owner's phone: the day's sales, top sellers and who was short at Mama Lishe Grill, then the Analytics page with its Do this next cards, then every branch side by side on a laptop." />
    <figcaption>The owner's view: the day on the phone, then every branch side by side. Demo businesses are fictional.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Make the second branch a copy of the first: items, prices, roles and the close.</li>
    <li>Ten minutes every night: sales, drawers by name, voids and refunds, what is running low.</li>
    <li>Every week: move stock before you buy it, count the expensive items, talk numbers with the person in charge.</li>
    <li>Give partners and accountants their own logins.</li>
    <li>Visit without warning once a month, and compare what you see with what the numbers say.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A second branch should double what you earn, not what you worry about. The routine is what makes the difference;
    the right point of sale makes the routine take ten minutes instead of an evening.
  </p>
</Guide>
